Article 7 A business operator shall not engage in the following confusing acts that cause a person to mistake its commodities for those of another person or to believe that there is a specific connection with another person:
(1) without authorization, using a mark identical or similar to another person's commodity name, packaging, decoration, etc. that has certain influence;
(2) without authorization, using another person's name (including abbreviation, trade name, etc.), personal name (including pen name, stage name, screen name, translated name, etc.) that has certain influence;
(3) without authorization, using another person's domain name main part, website name, web page, new media account name, application name or icon, etc. that has certain influence;
(4) other confusing acts sufficient to cause a person to mistake its commodities for those of another person or to believe that there is a specific connection with another person.
Where, without authorization, a business operator uses another person's registered trademark or unregistered well-known trademark as a trade name in its enterprise name, or sets another person's commodity name, enterprise name (including abbreviation, trade name, etc.), registered trademark, or unregistered well-known trademark as a search keyword, causing a person to mistake its commodities for those of another person or to believe that there is a specific connection with another person, such act falls within the confusing acts specified in the preceding paragraph.
A business operator shall not assist another person in engaging in confusing acts.

Article 10 A business operator shall not commit the following acts of infringing trade secrets:
(1) acquiring a trade secret of the right holder by theft, bribery, fraud, coercion, electronic intrusion or any other improper means;
(2) disclosing, using or allowing another person to use a trade secret of the right holder acquired by the means specified in the preceding subparagraph;
(3) disclosing, using or allowing another person to use a trade secret in its possession in violation of a confidentiality obligation or the right holder's requirements for keeping the trade secret confidential;
(4) abetting, enticing or assisting another person to acquire, disclose, use or allow another person to use a trade secret of the right holder in violation of a confidentiality obligation or the right holder's requirements for keeping the trade secret confidential.
Where a natural person, legal person or unincorporated organization other than a business operator commits the illegal acts specified in the preceding paragraph, it shall be deemed as an infringement of trade secrets.
Where a third party, while knowing or having reason to know that an employee, former employee or other entity or individual of the trade secret right holder has committed an illegal act specified in paragraph 1 of this Article, still acquires, discloses, uses, or allows another person to use the trade secret, it shall be deemed as an infringement of trade secrets.
For the purposes of this Law, "trade secrets" means technical information, operational information, or other commercial information that is unknown to the public, has commercial value, and for which the right holder has taken corresponding confidentiality measures.

Article 11 A business operator shall not have any of the following circumstances when conducting a prize-giving sale:
(1) the information on the type of prizes, conditions for claiming prizes, amount of prize money or prizes, or other prize-giving sale information is unclear, affecting the claiming of prizes;
(2) after the prize-giving sale activity commences, changing the type of prizes, conditions for claiming prizes, amount of prize money or prizes, or other prize-giving sale information without justifiable reasons;
(3) conducting the prize-giving sale by deceptive means such as falsely claiming that there are prizes or intentionally allowing a predetermined person to win a prize;
(4) in a lottery-type prize-giving sale, the amount of the top prize exceeds 50,000 yuan.

Article 13 Where a business operator engages in production and business operations on the Internet, it shall comply with the provisions of this Law.

A business operator shall not, by using data, algorithms, technology, platform rules, etc., or by influencing users' choices or by other means, commit any of the following acts that hinder or disrupt the normal operation of network products or services lawfully provided by other business operators:
(1) without the consent of other business operators, inserting links or forcing a target jump in the network products or services lawfully provided by them;
(2) misleading, deceiving, or coercing users into modifying, closing, or uninstalling network products or services lawfully provided by other business operators;
(3) maliciously implementing incompatibility with network products or services lawfully provided by other business operators;
(4) other acts that hinder or disrupt the normal operation of network products or services lawfully provided by other business operators.

A business operator shall not, by fraudulent, coercive, or other improper means such as circumventing or destroying technical management measures, acquire or use data lawfully held by other business operators, thereby harming the lawful rights and interests of other business operators and disrupting the order of market competition.

A business operator shall not abuse platform rules to directly or instruct another person to commit acts such as bogus transactions, false evaluations, or malicious returns against other business operators, thereby harming the lawful rights and interests of other business operators and disrupting the order of market competition.

Article 16 When investigating suspected acts of unfair competition, the supervision and inspection departments may take the following measures:
(1) entering the business premises where the suspected act of unfair competition occurs for inspection;
(2) questioning the business operator under investigation, interested parties, and other relevant entities and individuals, and requiring them to explain the relevant circumstances or provide other materials related to the act under investigation;
(3) consulting and copying agreements, account books, vouchers, documents, records, business correspondence, and other materials related to the suspected act of unfair competition;
(4) sealing up or seizing property and articles related to the suspected act of unfair competition;
(5) inquiring into the bank accounts of the business operator suspected of committing the act of unfair competition.
Measures specified in the preceding paragraph shall be taken only after a written report is made to and approved by the principal responsible person of the supervision and inspection department. Measures specified in items (4) and (5) of the preceding paragraph shall be taken only after a written report is made to and approved by the principal responsible person of the supervision and inspection department of the people's government at or above the city with districts.
When investigating suspected acts of unfair competition, the supervision and inspection departments shall comply with the provisions of the Administrative Compulsion Law of the People's Republic of China and other relevant laws and administrative regulations, and shall, in accordance with law, timely disclose the results of investigation and punishment to the public.

Article 39 In civil adjudication proceedings concerning trade secret infringement, where the trade secret right holder provides prima facie evidence proving that it has taken confidentiality measures for the alleged trade secret and reasonably indicates that the trade secret has been infringed, the alleged infringer shall prove that the trade secret claimed by the right holder does not fall within the trade secrets defined in this Law.
Where the trade secret right holder provides prima facie evidence reasonably showing that the trade secret has been infringed and provides any of the following evidence, the alleged infringer shall prove that it has not committed trade secret infringement:
(1) Evidence showing that the alleged infringer has a channel or opportunity to acquire the trade secret, and the information it uses is substantially identical to the trade secret;
(2) Evidence showing that the trade secret has been disclosed, used, or is at risk of being disclosed or used by the alleged infringer;
(3) Other evidence showing that the trade secret has been infringed by the alleged infringer.
